{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01V8NFCMKyHSThhGJw7yeawQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I have enough information to proceed with the review now. Based on multiple searches, I know CoRL uses the typical ML conference review format. Let me use the OpenReview default scale and what I know about CoRL's reviewing criteria. The typical CoRL review form uses a 1-10 scale similar to other ML conferences:\n\n1: Trivial or wrong\n2: Strong reject\n3: Clear reject\n4: Reject\n5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n7: Accept\n8: Strong accept\n9: Top 15% of accepted papers\n10: Top 5% of accepted papers\n\nCoRL's acceptance rate is around 25-30%.\n\n\nNow I'll structure this as a full review with two reviewers handling different aspects. R1 will focus on the methods and experimental design while R2 examines the novelty and positioning relative to existing work. I'll walk through the independent review phase, then have them cross-share their assessments, converge on key points, and synthesize into a final recommendation. The paper presents a diagnostic framework for analyzing how vision-language-action models process tokens across three different VLA families tested on four language instruction benchmarks. Now I'm setting up the review structure with two separate reviewers—one focusing on the methodological rigor and experimental completeness, the other on how novel the framework is and whether it matters for the robot learning community. I'll work through the roundtable discussion where they hash out their disagreements and converge on a final assessment.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2097,"cache_read_input_tokens":69271,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2097},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inf